A||||15||Hartz||Antenna base broke||||9||
Z||000000||Hartz||10-22-2001||05:42 PM||||When I went to remove my antenna, the male adapter post from the antenna base snapped off.  A couple of questions for anyone who might know:<P>1)  Do I HAVE to remove the fender to get at the base?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_dizzy.gif"><P>2)  Is the base a nice, generic piece, or is it specific to the Fiero?  (e.g. Is this available at Pep Boys, or do I have to scrounge?)<P>Any advice appreciated before I start tearing things apart!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Thanks,<BR>Hartz<P>'84 SC 5.7L V8<BR>'85 SE 3.1L V6 Turbo<P><BR>||||reg||9
Z||000001||MinnGreenGT||10-22-2001||05:56 PM||||Well, bad news on both accounts.  To my knowelege, yes you MUST remove the fender.  And yes, the antenna base is specific to the Fiero.  <P>Maybe it's time to install an electric antenna...?  Or increase your CD or cassette collection  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Good Luck!||||reg||1
Z||000002||Hartz||10-22-2001||06:03 PM||||Well, that was what it kind of looked like from the manual, but it never hurts to ask.  I was hoping...<P>Thanks for the reply.<P>Hartz<P>'84 SC 5.7L V8<BR>'85 SE 3.1L V6 Turbo<BR>hartzfeld.home.mindspring.com||||reg||1
Z||000003||Cheever3000||10-22-2001||06:11 PM||||I have seen a Fiero with a power antennae from a 300ZX, and it apparently fit nicely.  Looked good too.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||sqoach||10-22-2001||06:21 PM||||Yep, need to take off the fender. It isn't too hard. I've had my fender off in 15 minutes. You'll need a 10mm socket, 7mm socket, and I believe that's it. If you need another base, I have one on my SE. I plan on getting some kind of enternal intenna or something, so I won't need it. I'm not exactly sure how to get it out, though, but it shouldn't be too hard. LMK.<P>------------------<BR>-Adam<BR><FONT COLOR="BLUE">sqoach@yahoo.com</FONT><BR>              <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sqoach.gif">             <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">              <BR>^^^^^<B>new project</B>^^^^^<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><LI>86 SC 2.5L 5spd</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Monkeyman||10-22-2001||07:02 PM||||You can get a brand new antenna base for about 15 bucks from the Fiero Store (and probably from the dealer, too).  <P>Anyone have any info on adding a power antenna?  I've done a search and can't seem to find anything.  I think this is the way I'll go sooner or later.||||reg||1
Z||000006||mbramble||10-22-2001||07:18 PM||||I have added a power antenna to my 85 GT.  It is mounted under the right front fender and comes up thru the fender a few inches back from where the original came thru.  I filled the original hole w/fiberglass and drilled a new one.  It is one of those universal aftermarket ones you get from the AutoZone, etc.  I think it was $39. I still need to figure out someway to dress up the hole -- right now it is just a round hole in the fender.  But I still have more important things to take care of before I woory too much about that. The top of the antenna is below the fender.  It's not like mounting one to a metal fender...  The only photo I have that shows under the fender is a slide.  I will try to get it made into a print and scan it soon.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Old Lar||10-22-2001||07:43 PM||||Since I too broke the antenna base, I was told you don't have to completely remove the fender, but loosen the bolts holding it on, removing only the ones near the antenna, then pull the fender away to access the antenna base. Unbolt that d replace with a new one.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fierostore.com" TARGET=_blank>www.fierostore.com</A>   part # 61602 $15.95. For base and cable.<p>[This message has been edited by Old Lar (edited 10-22-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||roadwarrior||10-22-2001||09:26 PM||||hey if you guys want, I've got a spare base.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Black86SE||10-22-2001||11:53 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by mbramble:<BR><B>I have added a power antenna to my 85 GT.  It is mounted under the right front fender and comes up thru the fender a few inches back from where the original came thru.  I filled the original hole w/fiberglass and drilled a new one.  It is one of those universal aftermarket ones you get from the AutoZone, etc.  I think it was $39. I still need to figure out someway to dress up the hole -- right now it is just a round hole in the fender.  But I still have more important things to take care of before I woory too much about that. The top of the antenna is below the fender.  It's not like mounting one to a metal fender...  The only photo I have that shows under the fender is a slide.  I will try to get it made into a print and scan it soon.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>To dress up the hole just go to your local hardware and get a rubber grommet. Measure the size of the hole and find the grommet with the correct size. The grommet will have 3 different diameters, the grove diameter should be the size of your hole. Hope this makes sense.<P><BR>Hartz, if you just pull back the fender to get at the base, just be careful not to crack the fender. I did this once on a fiero in a junk yard. Just a little advice.<BR>------------------<BR>1986 V6 SE<BR>Black<BR>17" Wheels<BR>P235/45/ZR17 <BR>BF Goodrich g-Force KDWS<p>[This message has been edited by Black86SE (edited 10-22-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||Hartz||10-23-2001||12:53 AM||||Thanks everyone for the advice.  Getting the fender off was a LOT easier than I feared it would be.  And, as a bonus, a fellow PFF member who lives one town over has a new base, so I should be back in business shortly.<P>One interesting side note - you wouldn't believe the size of the hornet's nest that was attached inside the fender.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif">  Amazing.  I have no idea how long it's been in there...<P>Hartz||||reg||7
